messages.reasoning_content:
The help output:
```
Usage: ./executable [options]

Options:
    -d, --default TEXT  text inserted into the textfield on startup
    -o, --out-file FILE write final command to file
        --in-file FILE  read initial command from file
        --config-reference 
                        print out the default configuration file
    -r, --raw-mode      keep linebreaks in finished command when closing
        --no-isolation  disable isolation. This will run the commands directly
                        on your system, without protection. Take care.
    -h, --help          print this help menu
```
And `-d=x` → the command is "=x" ✓ (the short form takes the rest of the arg after the flag).

Let me check the exact column layout:
- "    -d, --default TEXT  text inserted into the textfield on startup": 4 spaces + "-d, --default TEXT" (18 chars) + 2 spaces + the description. So the flag column ends at 4+18 = 22, then 2 spaces → the description starts at column 24.
- "    -o, --out-file FILE write final command to file": 4 + "-o, --out-file FILE" (19) + 1 space + desc → hmm! "-o, --out-file FILE" is 19 chars, so 4+19 = 23, then the description starts at 24 ✓ (1 space).
- "        --in-file FILE  read initial command from file": 8 spaces + "--in-file FILE" (14) = 22 + 2 spaces → desc at 24 ✓.
- "        --config-reference " → 8 + "--config-reference" (18) = 26, then a space? Hmm, the line ends with a space ✓ ("--config-reference $" with a trailing space). Hmm! So the line is "        --config-reference " (8 + 18 + 1 space = 27 chars) and then the description is on the NEXT line (indented by 24 spaces).

Hmm, so getopts computes the description column as max over all options of (flag width) + 1... Hmm, but for --config-reference the flag width is 18 (starting at col 8) → 26, and the description would start at 27. Hmm, but the description is on the next line at column 24. Hmm!

So getopts' alignment: the description column is fixed (24 here) and if the flag part exceeds it, the description goes to the next line with the description indent.

Hmm, getopts' `usage()` implementation: it computes the row width for each option: "    " + flags + " " + desc, where the flags part is padded. Let me recall getopts' algorithm:

```rust
let desc_sep = format!("\n{}", repeat(" ").take(width).collect::<String>());
let any_short = ...;
let mut rows = vec![];
for opt in &opts {
